I can give you my take on this incident.  I'm not claiming
that it's THE TRUTH, since I didn't pay much attention to
the incident when it happened.

A prof killed himself and the campus newspapers report that
he killed himself.  There was some speculation as to why
(health reasons, etc.), but nothing really solid.  Later,
there was a story (can't remember if in the student
newspapers or the official staff newspaper, probably one of
the former) that a man in his 20s was claiming that he, as a
teenager, had been molested by the prof.  Apparently the
prof had been informed of these claims a short while before
he killed himself.  I did see some remarks by coworkers,
etc. that they couldn't believe he molested the teenager.
(IMO, a fairly typical response when a "pillar of the
community" is accused of molestation.)

I haven't seen any reports of evidence that the prof was a
pedophile, other than the claims of the young man.  (I'm not
saying he's lying, just that I haven't read of anything but
his claims.)  IMO, most of the staff and coworkers gave the
dead prof the benefit of a doubt and continued to treat his
memory as him as a kind, decent prof.  I haven't heard about
anything being named after him.

I can't help but wonder if by this criterion the Roman
Catholic Church isn't PC, since it has done some massive
denying that priests are molesting children within the
Church.  


