In article <CF03MB.HD@ryn.mro4.dec.com> sherman@sofbas.enet.dec.com (My name is...) writes:

>o U of Cal. for enforcing a policy of student admission on a strict racial
>quota basis, regardless of personal qualification or capability

As a Berkeley alumni, I feel duty bound to clarify any misconception that
might arise for this overly simplistic/simple-minded reduction of the UC
system.  Mind you, I'm not *defending* it, as I'm not entirely comfortable
with it myself; but I do feel it needs a few more words than this...
(what follows is _my_ best understanding of the situation)

Okay, first off _admission_ to the UC system *IS* on merit with NO
racial quotas.  But that gets you into the `system', which is made up
of a number of campuses (Berkeley, L.A., Riverside, Irvine, Davis...).
Now, some campuses are more popular than others for a variety of reasons
(better reputation, better social environment, ... whatever) and thus
more students choose it as their "campus of choice" than it can actually
accomodate.  Now if the more popular campuses (Berkeley being the
"flagship" and best :-), and thus most popular) were to accecpt out of the
UC pool (all accepted students are guarenteed entry into at least ONE
campus) by skimming strictly off the top, then the student population
would be quite odd and in no way reflect that of the general population.
This is where the quotas come into play...to equalize the racial mixture
to more accurately reflect the makeup of CA at-large.

Now, all this is "unfair" to those best-of-the-best students that don't
get to go to their first choice campus though other less (but not
UNqualified) students of a different race do get to attend at that
campus.  But one should note that the students are not -excluded- from
getting a UC education.  Maybe this subtlety to too fine for some....

