This is one of the most mindless analogies I have read on Usenet.

The purpose of subsidizing education is to produce graduates, who will
(one hopes) go on to do more with their lives than they otherwise would
have without a degree.  Spending limited funds on those who have a very
poor chance of showing up for the cap & gown ceremony is just stupid.

The purpose of subsidizing food is to eliminate hunger, malnutrition &
starvation.  Purchasing gourmet groceries with these limited funds is
inexcusably wasteful.

Please note that these are two very different purposes.

>Some Detail:
>
>Why use test scores at all? The only "fair" entrance criteria is
>ability to pay.

If that was true anyone could buy their way into Harvard, which is not
true (yet).

>If you do decide to give out state subsidies for education, why give
>it *exclusively* to the students who will do best? It makes more sense
>to give a state subsidy to those it will help the most. It also makes
>sense to try to distribute the subsidies to all groups in the state.
>Would you exclude road funds from Watts just because BWMs and Jags of
>Beverly Hills will go the fastest on really fine roads?

This makes as much sense as your previous attempt at reasoning.  There
are no roads in the US, at the local, state or federal level, which have
been optimized for the performance characteristics of expensive European
sports cars.  Any analysis of the tax revenue vs. spending pattern for
poor neighborhoods shows a subsidy from the wealthier parts of town.
This is entirely in line with the attempt to provide a common minimal
level of services to everyone.  And everyone can benefit from better
roads, while only a select few can benefit from an education at an elite
university. 




