I was there when it happened.  

Essentially, someone in the Black Student Union noticed a windmill to
tilt at, organized some protests, and the (undergraduate) Student
Association voted to ask the university to ban blood drives on campus.

The Red Cross policy was that 'Sub-Saharan Africans' were asked not to
donate blood.  The policy wasn't the Red Cross's idea, it was part of
the standard set by the government (I think it's Food and Drug
Administration) so the Red Cross had to do what it said.

The conservative newspaper took the opportunity to work up righteous
indignation that someone would monkey with the blood supply for Our
Brave Boys in Desert Storm(tm).  The liberal newspaper took the
opportunity to work up righteous indignation that someone would ever
do anything that could be interpreted as racist.

The university administration temporarily banned blood drives so they
could 'study' the problem, and waited until the following semester,
when the FDA changed the wording of its policy in a weaselly way so it
didn't look like it did before and the blood drives could start again.

It's amazing how student-government politics resembles a flamewar on
USENET, except that on USENET people use better logic.

They also ask you not to donate if you've had sex with a prostitute,
if you've traveled to various countries with lousy sanitation, and
various other things.  All they do is ask you, and it's not clear what
a 'Sub-Saharan African' is -- at the very least it's probably someone
who lives in Africa, which nobody at SUNY Binghamton does, because
the commute is too long.

I think the reason for FDA's policy was that the test for the HIV II
virus, which is apparently the strain endemic to Africa, is more
expensive or less reliable or something, so they wanted to exclude as
many donors who had been exposed to the virus as possible.

